## Why This Is Asked
Tests that cover client-side persistence and recovery from autosave are common in product forms. This checks basic E2E skills without server integration.

## Key Concepts
- E2E testing of client-side autosave via localStorage
- Time control with cy.clock and cy.tick
- Form state restoration after reload
- Cleanup of autosave after completion

## Code Example
```javascript
describe('Profile Wizard Autosave', () => {
  it('restores autosaved progress after reload', () => {
    cy.clock();
    cy.visit('/profile/new');
    cy.get('#firstName').type('Alex');
    cy.tick(120000);
    cy.window().then(win => {
      const data = JSON.parse(win.localStorage.getItem('profile_wizard'));
      expect(data?.firstName).to.eq('Alex');
    });
    cy.reload();
    cy.get('#firstName').should('have.value', 'Alex');
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this test for multiple locales or browsers?
- How would you handle autosave failures or corrupted localStorage data?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's understanding of end-to-end testing for multi-step user journeys, including authentication flows, external system mocking, and deterministic test data management. It evaluates practical knowledge of creating reliable, fast, and isolated tests in a real-world SaaS context.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing with authentication and email verification
- Test data seeding and database isolation
- Mocking external services (email delivery, OIDC sign-in)
- Assertions for UI state and created resources
- Time-bounded test design for CI/CD pipelines

## Code Example

```javascript
// Using Playwright
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('complete onboarding flow', async ({ page }) => {
  // Seed deterministic test data
  await seedTestUser({ email: 'demo@test.com', password: 'Test123!' });

  // Mock email service to capture verification link
  await mockEmailService();

  // Mock OIDC provider
  await mockOIDCProvider();

  // Navigate to signup and complete registration
  await page.goto('/signup');
  await page.fill('[name=email]', 'demo@test.com');
  await page.fill('[name=password]', 'Test123!');
  await page.click('[data-testid=signup-submit]');

  // Extract and follow email verification link
  const verifyLink = await getVerificationLink('demo@test.com');
  await page.goto(verifyLink);

  // Complete profile setup
  await page.fill('[name=name]', 'Jane Doe');
  await page.fill('[name=company]', 'Acme Corp');
  await page.click('[data-testid=profile-submit]');

  // Create first workspace
  await page.fill('[name=workspaceName]', 'My Workspace');
  await page.click('[data-testid=create-workspace]');

  // Assert dashboard displays new workspace
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid=workspace-card]')).toContainText('My Workspace');
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for per-user feature flags are common in SaaS apps. This question checks the ability to seed data, perform API-driven state changes, and verify UI behavior across login and page reloads in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Per-user feature flags and API seed/reset
- End-to-end UI verification of dynamic layouts
- Test isolation and deterministic CI runs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example using Playwright-like syntax (pseudocode)
// seedUser(), setFlag(), login() are helper utilities
test('compact dashboard toggling', async ({ page, request }) => {
  const { userId, email, password } = await seedUser(request);
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', true);
  await login(page, email, password);
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', false);
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-full')).toBeVisible();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you guard against flaky UI assertions caused by caching?
- How would you extend the test to cover multiple user segments (roles/regions) and parallel execution?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of end-to-end testing for real-time media systems at scale, with emphasis on synchronization verification, fault injection, and observability under production-like conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Large-scale WebRTC testing with synthetic clients
- Network emulation including latency, jitter, and packet loss profiles
- Audio-video synchronization verification
- Recording integrity and consistency across media servers
- Burst load testing and stress testing methodologies
- Signaling and media path coordination

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for WebRTC E2E test harness
class WebRTCE2EHarness {
  constructor(config) {
    this.rooms = [];
    this.networkProfiles = config.networkProfiles;
    this.statsInterval = config.statsIntervalMs || 1000;
  }

  async createRoom(participants, networkProfile) {
    const room = new Room(participants, networkProfile);
    await room.initialize();
    this.rooms.push(room);
    return room;
  }

  async runBurstLoadTest(options) {
    const { roomCount, participantsPerRoom, staggerDelay } = options;
    for (let i = 0; i < roomCount; i++) {
      await this.createRoom(participantsPerRoom, this.getRandomProfile());
      await this.sleep(staggerDelay);
    }
  }

  collectMetrics() {
    return this.rooms.flatMap(room => room.getRTCStats());
  }

  verifyRecordingSync(recording1, recording2) {
    const drift = Math.abs(recording1.timestamp - recording2.timestamp);
    return drift < 50; // 50ms tolerance
  }
}
```

